**Definition 1.1 (the sufficient interface).** The graph $G_{\rm sel}$ defined
below is installed at original initialization on ARC's 74 roles. It keeps
the outer menu $00,01,1$. Only inside the admitted history branch $1$ it
acquires a separate bit $\mu$, called HistoryMode. Mode zero runs the old
history-frame parser. Mode one acquires a frame with one of two payloads:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{AcquisitionOffset}(t)&=0\,U(t),\\
\operatorname{SourceOffset}(m,t)&=1\,U(m)\,U(t),\qquad U(n)=1^n0.
\end{aligned}
$$

For an authentic acquired cover with acquisition and predecessor tallies
$a,b$, the first payload binds $k=a+t$ when $t\le b-a$. The second finds
the actual completed auxiliary source occurrence with old unified ordinal
$m$, constructs its entry and exit tallies $e,f$ internally, and binds
$k=e+t$ when $t\le f-e$. Both feed ARC's existing immutable Issue and
received-reference Call. AcquisitionOffset alone suffices for every cut of
$[a,b]$; SourceOffset supplies an actual source-occurrence adapter without
asking its requester for an absolute primitive tally. These are two arms of
one installed program, with one common cover, source, codec and consumer.

